## Summary

Completed **1.2a — Pure refactor first**: moved `apply_batch_selections` (the bulk-prep/long-cook batch applier) from `ui_generation.py` into `week.py`, the shared planning module, and wired it into `planner.generate_and_store_week` — the function CLI and API both already funnel through — so all three front ends now call the identical function at the identical point (after location modes, before `resolve_auto_choices`/`validate_week`/the first model call).

**Key changes:**
- `src/week.py`: added `apply_batch_selections` right after `spread_batch` (its only real dependency besides `fridge_day_gaps`), plus a small `DEFAULT_BATCH_TARGET_SERVINGS` fallback constant — needed because `week.py` sits *below* `planner.py` in the dependency graph (planner imports from week, never the reverse), so it can't call `planner.planning_rule` directly.
- `src/ui_generation.py`: deleted the local definition, now imports the same function object from `week` (and dropped the imports/typing names that were only used inside it).
- `src/planner.py`: `generate_and_store_week` now calls `apply_batch_selections` and merges the anchors into `config`, exactly mirroring what the UI path already did — a no-op for CLI/API today since `bulk_prep_enabled`/`long_cook_enabled` are review-dialog-only staged fields, but now running through the same code.

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
The trickiest part of this "pure" refactor wasn't the logic — it was the module boundary. `week.py` is deliberately the dependency-free, API-free layer that `planner.py` builds on (per CLAUDE.md's architecture section), so a function that used `planner.planning_rule()` couldn't just be copy-pasted down a level without creating an import cycle. The fix — a locally-owned fallback constant, mirroring `PlanningRules.batch_target_servings`'s default — follows the same precedent the codebase already set for `DEFAULT_STORAGE_WINDOWS` (owned by `week.py`, imported upward by `planner.py`'s Pydantic model).

I also verified the move didn't just *relocate* the function but truly shares it: `wk.apply_batch_selections is ui_generation.apply_batch_selections is planner.apply_batch_selections` — one object, three names pointing at it. That's a cheap, precise way to enforce "import, never duplicate" as a test assertion rather than just a code-review habit.
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

